{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he said, I will certainly return to you.</b> This is the angel who came to bring tidings to Sarah, and he spoke thus, and his word was fulfilled in the good tidings of a son in the coming year. But we do not find that he returned, as he said to him twice שוב אשוב — 'I will certainly return' — למועד אשוב אליך — 'at the appointed time I will return to you.' Perhaps the angel's intention was not that he himself would return to him, but rather that his word would be fulfilled and come as 'a living time' (כעת חיה), and Scripture calls the fulfillment of the angel's word his 'return,' for what would his returning accomplish if he were not to bring tidings of a son? Or we may say it is from the root of (Isaiah 30:15) ״בשובה ונחת״, whose sense is the resting of the spirit — that is, 'I will rest your mind and have compassion upon you with a son in the coming year,' for this angel was appointed over mercy." "<b>At a living time.</b> At the fixed time of a woman who gives birth, which is twelve months, for the time of nine months is not fixed — sometimes it is early or late — but the time that does not delay and go late beyond is twelve months, and that is the fixed time. As is said in the case of Rabba Tosfa'a (Yevamot 2a). Therefore the angel gave him now, at Passover, a fixed time: that he would have a son at the coming Passover." "<b>And he was behind him.</b> Scripture tells that Sarah, who was sitting inside the tent — as it is written, 'behold, in the tent' — rose from her place to hear the words, to the entrance of the tent, and the heralding angel was behind the entrance of the tent."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:18.789776992Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}